#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# function_sign

#% text
# `function_sign(f, x)` gives `1` when `f >= 0` for every `x` of its domain, `-1` when `f <= 0`, `0` when `f == 0`, and `indeterminate` otherwise.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The sign is proved from the facts of the domain where it can be (`refine`); otherwise, for one variable without parameters, it is found by sampling the domain: a change of sign among the samples gives `indeterminate`.

#% item
# `function_sign([f, cond], x)` restricts the domain.

#% item
# `function_sign(f, n, integers)` for a sequence.

#% item
# A function not defined on the whole real line gives `indeterminate` unless a condition restricts the domain.

#% item
# With parameters, several variables or `strict_inequalities: true` the sign of a polynomial (or rational) function is decided by the cylindrical decomposition: `conditional_expression(1, c)` with the condition on the parameters; `generate_conditions` and `assumptions` as for `function_continuous`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# An absolute value and a sine:

#% cell
function_sign(abs(x), x)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
function_sign(sin(x), x)
#% output
#= indeterminate

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A shifted sine:

#% cell
function_sign(sin(x) + 3/2, x)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# On a restricted domain:

#% cell
function_sign([sin(x), 0 <= x and x <= pi], x)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# A difference that is never positive:

#% cell
function_sign(x - sqrt(x^2), x)
#% output
#= -1

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# A quadratic with parameters:

#% cell
function_sign(x^2 + a*x + b, x)
#% output
#= conditional_expression(1, -4*b + a^2 <= 0)

#% text
# Two variables:

#% cell
[function_sign(x^2 + a*x*y + y^2, [x, y]), function_sign(x*y, [x, y])]
#% output
#= [conditional_expression(1, -2 <= a and a <= 2), indeterminate]

#% text
# Strict inequalities:

#% cell
map(&(function_sign(&1, x, strict_inequalities: true)), [x^2, x^2 + 1])
#% output
#= [indeterminate, 1]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `function_monotonicity` is the sign of the derivative:

#% cell
function_monotonicity(x^3, x)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
function_sign(d(x^3, x), x)
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Without a proof the answer comes from samples of the domain; a sign change between samples very close together can be missed:

#% cell
function_sign(x^2 - 10^-8, x)
#% output
#= indeterminate

#% text
# A square root is not defined on the whole line, so its sign is indeterminate unless the domain is restricted:

#% cell
[function_sign(sqrt(x), x), function_sign([sqrt(x), x > 0], x)]
#% output
#= [indeterminate, 1]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `function_monotonicity`, `function_convexity`, `refine`, `sign`
